---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const industries = [
  {
    title: 'Drones & robotics',
    summary: 'Perception integrity with a real safety gate in the control loop, not a log line after the fact.',
    points: [
      'Reliability score gates the controller directly, a confirmed fault can hold position or pause before a bad inference is ever acted on.',
      'Runs alongside real telemetry (GPS quality, battery, IMU, joint torque) as additional independent evidence.',
      'Local self-healing works without a persistent uplink, a disconnected unit keeps detecting, buffering, and recovering, and syncs when it reconnects.',
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'Defense & ISR',
    summary: "Black-box by design: your model's weights, gradients, and internals never have to leave your control.",
    points: [
      "Decision-making reads only a model's outputs, embeddings, boxes, confidence, so it can watch systems you can't or won't expose.",
      'Weight-integrity hashing and rollback ladders give an auditable, tamper-evident trail for every model state a unit has run.',
      'Escalation-over-looping: a persistent fault raises a human alert instead of retrying indefinitely, which matters when the cost of a wrong autonomous action is high.',
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'Industrial automation',
    summary: 'Local self-healing keeps a line or a warehouse fleet moving without a round trip to the cloud.',
    points: [
      'Fine-tune, validate, and hot-reload happen in-process, on real buffered data from the actual operating environment.',
      'A bounded, validated rollback ladder means a bad update can always be reverted to the last known-good state.',
      "Fleet-wide learning is optional, not required, a single unit is fully capable of detecting and healing on its own.",
    ],
  },
];

const alsoFor = [
  { title: 'Autonomous vehicles & maritime', body: 'Perception and control models where a wrong decision has no undo.' },
  { title: 'Smart infrastructure & energy', body: 'Grid, water, and building-automation models operating unattended for months.' },
  { title: 'Logistics & warehouse robotics', body: 'Fleets of pickers, sorters, and AMRs that must keep moving through drift.' },
  { title: 'Agricultural robotics', body: 'Field AI running for entire seasons with no connectivity guarantee.' },
  { title: 'Financial & risk models', body: 'Decision models where a silent drift is a compliance event, not just a bug.' },
  { title: 'Any inference-serving system', body: "If it emits an embedding, a confidence score, and a decision, it's a fit." },
];
---

<BaseLayout title="Industries" description="Where a silent AI failure is not an acceptable outcome, in any kind of system.">
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 pt-20 pb-16 sm:pt-28 sm:pb-20 border-b border-ink-600">
    <p class="eyebrow">Industries</p>
    <h1 class="mt-5 max-w-3xl font-display text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-semibold leading-tight">
      Any AI-based system that has to keep working, and fail safely, unsupervised.
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl text-lg text-paper-300">
      The pattern is the same everywhere it matters most: an AI model making decisions far from a human
      operator, where a silent failure is more dangerous than a loud one. The architecture is deliberately
      not built around any one sensor type, output format, or industry, it's built around the shape of the
      problem, which shows up almost everywhere machine judgment drives a real-world action.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-20 sm:py-28 space-y-20">
    <p class="eyebrow -mb-10">Where we've validated the deepest</p>
    {
      industries.map((ind) => (
        <div class="grid gap-8 md:grid-cols-[0.8fr_1.2fr] md:gap-16">
          <div>
            <h2 class="font-display text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-semibold leading-tight">{ind.title}</h2>
            <p class="mt-4 text-paper-300">{ind.summary}</p>
          </div>
          <ul class="space-y-4">
            {ind.points.map((p) => (
              <li class="panel text-sm text-paper-300">{p}</li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </section>

  <section class="border-y border-ink-600 bg-ink-900/40">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-20 sm:py-28">
      <p class="eyebrow">Also a fit, by design not by accident</p>
      <h2 class="mt-4 max-w-2xl font-display text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-semibold leading-tight">
        Anywhere an AI model's output feeds a real decision.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl text-paper-300">
        We've built and validated the deepest against perception models on physical, moving systems, that's
        where a wrong decision is hardest to undo, so it's the strongest proof. The trust layer itself only
        needs three things from any model: an embedding or feature representation, a confidence or
        probability output, and a decision or prediction, which is a very short list to meet.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-14 grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
        {
          alsoFor.map((a) => (
            <div class="border-t border-ink-600 pt-5">
              <h3 class="font-display text-base font-semibold text-paper-100">{a.title}</h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-paper-500">{a.body}</p>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-ink-600">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-20 sm:py-28 text-center">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-semibold max-w-xl mx-auto">
        Don't see your exact use case? The architecture is deliberately domain-agnostic.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-paper-300 max-w-xl mx-auto">
        Anything that emits an embedding, a confidence score, and a bounding box, class, or decision can be
        monitored, regardless of sensor, model family, or programming language.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
        <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/contact`} class="btn-primary">Talk to us</a>
        <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/integrate`} class="btn-secondary">See the integration guide</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
